-- title:
--   Dichotomy against high eigenspaces forces kernel or finite dimension
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{K}$ be $\mathbb{R}$ or $\mathbb{C}$ (an `RCLike` field) and let $E$ be a complete normed additive commutative group carrying an inner product space structure over $\mathbb{K}$, i.e. a Hilbert space. Let $T : E \to E$ be a continuous $\mathbb{K}$-linear map which is a compact operator (`IsCompactOperator`) and whose underlying linear map is symmetric, $\langle Tx, y\rangle = \langle x, Ty\rangle$ for all $x,y$. Let $X$ be a $\mathbb{K}$-submodule of $E$, and write, for a real $r$, $E_{\ge r} = \bigsqcup_{\|\mu\| \ge r} \ker(T - \mu)$ for the submodule generated by the eigenspaces of $T$ at eigenvalues $\mu \in \mathbb{K}$ of norm at least $r$ (the supremum in the lattice of submodules). Assume that for every real $r > 0$ the subspace $X$ satisfies one of the two alternatives: either $X \cap E_{\ge r}^{\perp} = 0$, or $X \subseteq E_{\ge r}^{\perp}$. The conclusion is again a disjunction: either $X$ is contained in the kernel of $T$, or $X$ is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{K}$.
--
--   This is the dichotomy-to-finiteness form of the spectral theorem for compact self-adjoint operators: a subspace that for each threshold $r>0$ either meets the orthogonal complement of the high-eigenvalue part trivially or lies inside it is either annihilated by $T$ or finite-dimensional, since the high part $E_{\ge r}$ is finite-dimensional for $r>0$. It is used in the proof of finite-dimensionality of the space of vectors of fixed level and archimedean type in an irreducible cuspidal constituent, where the first alternative is excluded by an approximate identity.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ContinuousLinearMap_le_ker_or_finiteDimensional_of_forall_inf_highPart_orthogonal.lean

